Integrating PDF guidance retrieval into your existing EC2/SQLite architecture without adopting expensive managed vector stores is entirely straightforward. This design pattern is called Hybrid RAG (Structured + Unstructured).
1. Where to Store the PDFs
• The Source Files: Store the raw PDF documents in an AWS S3 bucket. This acts as your durable, central file repository.
• The Search Index: Instead of running a heavy cloud vector database, you can build a lightweight, local vector index directly on your EC2 instance using an embedded Python library like ChromaDB, FAISS, or SQLite-vss (an extension for SQLite).
Because your document corpus for retrofit guidelines is likely small-to-moderate, a local vector store runs entirely in-memory or as a local file on your EC2 instance for zero extra monthly infrastructure cost.
2. How to Integrate It into Your Python Architecture
You can expand your existing FastAPI/Python backend to handle both data types using a unified workflow.
Step-by-Step Data Processing Pipeline (Run on EC2)
1. Ingest & Chunk: Write a Python script that pulls the PDFs from S3, extracts the text (using libraries like pypdf or pymupdf), and splits them into logical paragraphs or "chunks" (e.g., 500 characters each).
2. Embed: Use AWS Bedrock’s embedding model (e.g., Amazon Titan Text Embeddings) via boto3 to convert each text chunk into a vector.
3. Store Locally: Save these vectors and text chunks into a local vector directory on your EC2 instance using ChromaDB.
import boto3
import chromadb
from pypdf import PdfReader

# Initialize local ChromaDB on EC2
chroma_client = chromadb.PersistentClient(path="./local_vector_db")
collection = chroma_client.get_or_create_collection(name="retrofit_docs")

# Bedrock client for embeddings
bedrock = boto3.client(service_name="bedrock-runtime", region_name="eu-west-2")


def get_embedding(text):
  response = bedrock.invoke_model(
      modelId="amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0",
      body=json.dumps({"inputText": text}),
  )
  return json.loads(response["body"].read())["embedding"]

3. Handling User Queries (The Unified Router Pattern)
When a user submits a question through your public interface, your Python application needs to decide whether the query requires structured database lookup (organisations, locations, counts) or unstructured document retrieval (regulations, advice, technical rules).
